# A patch file must reach the build byte for byte. Any line-ending conversion makes
# `git apply` fail to match the source, and the patch mechanism is deliberately loud --
# so a CRLF checkout on one platform would stop the build for a reason that has nothing
# to do with the patch.
*.patch -text

# A build script is executed by bash on a Linux runner. A CR at the end of every line
# makes `source function.sh` fail at its first `{`, and bash then reports the symptom
# -- "get_arch_name: command not found", four hundred times -- and never the cause.
#
# That is not hypothetical: the 8.1 security build of 2026-09-24 died that way. The
# scripts had been edited from a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true, where the
# working tree is CRLF by design; the conversion travelled into the commit.
#
# eol=lf makes the checkout LF on every platform, so what is edited is what runs.
*.sh text eol=lf
